CONFIRMED GEOMETRY PROBLEMS

1. NON-MODULAR PIECE PROPORTIONS

Two standard blocks placed side by side currently occupy approximately
three-quarters of one Timber Beam length.

The dimensions do not appear to express a deliberate modular relationship such
as:

- Two blocks equal one beam
- Three blocks equal one beam
- One block equals a documented fraction of a beam

This causes:

- Unpredictable span lengths
- Asymmetric structures
- Beam endpoints that do not align with block columns
- Roof peaks shifted away from center
- Repeated small gaps or overhangs
- Snap candidates that look close but fail collider validation

2. BLOCK CORNERS ARE NOT FIRST-CLASS CONNECTION FEATURES

An angled Timber Beam endpoint approaching the upper corner of a block does not
reliably receive the expected corner candidate.

The solver tends to prefer locations farther along broad flat faces.

The player expects the rafter endpoint to seat at the block corner.

3. VERTICAL-TO-HORIZONTAL CORNER CONNECTIONS ARE INCONSISTENT

A vertical Timber Beam approaching the end of a horizontal Timber Beam may:

- Attach outside the endpoint
- Shift inward along the top face
- Use an ambiguous T-joint
- Produce an offset or overhang
- Produce a different result depending on placement order

The builder should recognize a clean L-shaped frame corner.

4. ROOF PEAKS ARE ASYMMETRIC

Two equal and opposite angled rafters may approach one another, but their inward
corners do not reliably form a shared apex.

Observed outcomes include:

- Endpoint-center alignment rather than face/corner alignment
- One beam offset above or beside the other
- Visible square endpoint faces
- Small gaps
- Intersections
- An asymmetric peak despite symmetric supports and angles

5. LOWER RAFTER SUPPORTS ARE DIFFICULT TO EXPRESS

The Roof Peak joint may form while the lower rafter endpoints remain unconnected
or unsupported.

During stability testing, the roof then spreads or collapses.

The physics outcome may be correct, but the builder did not provide an
intuitive corner-seat connection at the lower supports.

